<h1>Johnson-Trotter</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Generate <a href="permutation.html"><em>permutations</em></a> by swapping one adjacent pair of elements at a time.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> Steinhaus-Johnson-Trotter, Trotter-Johnson.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="permutation.html"><em>permutation</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="fisherYatesShuffle.html"><em>Fisher-Yates shuffle</em></a>, <a href="graycode.html"><em>Gray code</em></a>, <a href="unrank.html"><em>unranking</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
<a href="https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/johnson-trotter-algorithm/" target="_blank">(C++, Java, C#)</a>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Hale F. Trotter</strong>, <a href="https://doi.acm.org/10.1145/368637.368660" target="_blank">Perm  (Algorithm 115)</a>, CACM, 5(8):434-435, August 1962. <br /> <strong>Selmer M. Johnson</strong>, <a href="http://www.jstor.org/stable/2003846" target="_blank">Generation of Permutations by Adjacent Transposition</a>, Mathematics of Computation, 17(83):282-285, July 1963.
</p>

